+# Substrate mapping: the reactivegas machine on kelgroups
+
+This page is the design record for issue
+[#47](https://github.com/paolino/reactivegas/issues/47): how the economic
+machine specified in [`lean/Reactivegas/`](https://github.com/paolino/reactivegas/tree/main/lean/Reactivegas)
+(see [State machine](state-machine.md)) plugs into the
+[kelgroups](https://github.com/paolino/kelgroups) substrate. It is the
+requirements input to paolino/kelgroups#28 (application API v2) and
+paolino/kelgroups#30 (app-scoped proposals); those tickets freeze their
+contracts against it.
+
+Standing rulings (epic [#43](https://github.com/paolino/reactivegas/issues/43),
+2026-08-25): the legacy `Eventi/` reactors are the specification; members are
+sovereign (every utente holds their own key); responsabili may still declare
+on behalf of a keyless member; deposits stay responsabile-authored because
+they record physical cash custody; insolvency is banned.
+
+The Lean machine is authoritative and unchanged by this mapping. Where the
+substrate seems to demand a semantics change, this document records an open
+question instead of an edit — see [Open questions](#open-questions).
+
+## Role mapping
+
+| legacy concept | kelgroups reality | evidence |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| responsabile | a member holding an admin role (`AdminRole`) | `KelGroups.Types.hasAdmin`, `KelGroups.State.isAdmin` |
+| utente | any group member (`members :: Map Text Member`) | `KelGroups.State.GroupState` |
+| referente of a collection | per-collection application state (`Collection.referente`), set from the opener at `openPurchase` | `Reactivegas.State.Collection`; legacy `Impegni.referente` in `Eventi/Impegno.hs` |
+| assenso majority | admin-majority proposals, `ceil(admins / 2)` | `KelGroups.State.majority`; legacy `maggioranza` in `Eventi/Anagrafe.hs` |
+
+There are **no application roles**: reactivegas registers no `RoleDef`
+entries (`roleDefs` stays empty, as in `KelGroups.Trivial`). Everything the
+legacy system expressed through roles is either an admin distinction
+(responsabile) or pure application state (referente). This keeps the base
+layer free of reactivegas vocabulary: `AppRole` exists in
+`KelGroups.Types` but the reactivegas configuration never uses it.
+
+Consequences:
+
+* **Admission** is owned by the base layer. `NuovoUtente`
+  (`Eventi/Anagrafe.hs`) becomes a base `IntroduceMember` proposal with a CESR
+  key; the app observes enactment as its `addUser` interface event. The Lean
+  guards (`!s.users.contains u`) mirror the base's own
+  `MemberAlreadyExists` check in `KelGroups.Validate`, so the app transition
+  cannot fail on provenance-respecting input. The proposal's CESR key is the
+  introduced member's own when they hold one; for a keyless target it is a
+  base-layer handle the target does not hold (see **Keylessness** below).
+* **Election and revocation** of responsabili are owned by the base layer:
+  `ChangeRoles` granting or revoking the admin role, decided by admin
+  majority — already native kelgroups machinery. The app observes enactment
+  as `electResponsabile` / `removeResponsabile`. Demotion is ordinary
+  **rotation, not sanction** — how responsabili come and go, routine turnover
+  of which members act for the group — with no emergency, hostile-cassiere or
+  deposition procedure attached; the same admin-majority vote that elects
+  also demotes. Revocation carries the L1/L4
+  economics: the removed responsabile's open collections are stripped and all
+  their pledges refunded (`stripCollections` + `refundAll`, theorems
+  `governance_enacts_remove`, `conservation_preserved`). This mirrors the
+  legacy internal event `EventoEliminazioneResponsabile`
+  (`Eventi/Anagrafe.hs`). What becomes of a departing responsabile's cassa is
+  owned by a future Lean amendment (settle-at-zero before removal, in either
+  sign) that is already routed ahead of the Haskell core and is out of scope
+  for this mapping; today's `removeResponsabile` has no balance precondition,
+  and nothing here depends on one.
+* **Bootstrap**: legacy `bootAnagrafe` seeds founding responsabili;
+  kelgroups bootstrap mode (zero admins, passphrase auth,
+  `KelGroups.Bootstrap.authMode`) requires the first `IntroduceMember` to
+  carry an admin role (`BootstrapRequiresAdmin` in `KelGroups.Validate`). The
+  app boot state is `State.init r` with `r` = the founding responsabile. One
+  founder plays both sides: first admin of the base group and initial state
+  of the app fold.
+* **Keylessness**: a keyless utente cannot sign anything themselves — they
+  hold no key — but they are not outside the machine. A responsabile admits
+  them through the same base `IntroduceMember` route as anyone else: the
+  responsabile proposes and signs, the proposal's CESR key is a base-layer
+  handle the utente does not hold, and the base records the member under
+  that handle. On enactment the app folds the `addUser a u` interface
+  event — author `a` is the proposing responsabile (`BaseOutcome` carries
+  the proposer), target `u` the fresh id — and `addUser` is the sole Lean
+  transition that appends to `State.users`; its guard,
+  `isResponsabile s a && !s.users.contains u`, mentions no key, so a
+  keyless target enters the users set exactly like a key-holding member.
+  Every later deposit or pledge naming them is authored on their behalf by
+  a responsabile (the epic ruling) and satisfies `s.users.contains u`. See
+  the AUTH matrix.
+
+## Assensi → proposals
+
+The legacy `programmazioneAssenso` / `programmazionePermesso` machinery in
+`Eventi/Anagrafe.hs` collected pros and cons (`Assensi richiedente [pros]
+[cons]`) or asked a single named responsabile (`Permesso richiedente
+interrogato`). The three legacy flows map onto three different kelgroups
+mechanisms:
+
+### Elections and revocations — base proposals (already native)
+
+Legacy election (`ElezioneResponsabile`) and revocation
+(`EliminazioneResponsabile`) ran `programmazioneAssenso` with the
+`maggioranza` threshold over elected responsabili. kelgroups base proposals
+(`Propose`/`Approve` over `ChangeRoles`) implement exactly this: proposed by
+an admin, approved by admin majority, enacted into the membership map. No
+application payload needed; the app reacts to the enactment
+([Interface events](#the-applogic-interface-contract)).
+
+### Purchase approval — app-scoped proposal (kelgroups#30)
+
+Opening a purchase in legacy code started *two* things: the pledge collection
+and a majority assenso whose positive outcome set the closure permission
+(`positivo -> ci` sets `permesso = True` in `Eventi/Acquisto.hs` /
+`Eventi/Impegno.hs`) and whose negative outcome failed the purchase with full
+refunds. This is law L2: closing needs group assent (`permissionToClose`),
+and denial is also a verdict — `denyPermission` refunds every pledge
+(`Step.lean`).
+
+Mapping: the purchase-approval question is an **app-scoped proposal** per
+kelgroups#30 carrying the payload "grant closure permission to collection
+*c*". Admins (= responsabili) vote exactly as for base proposals; the
+majority rule is unchanged, including single-admin immediate enactment
+(legacy behaves identically at one responsabile: `(1+1) div 2 = 1`). At
+enactment — whichever way the verdict falls — kelgroups injects an ordinary
+app event into the fold with KEL provenance:
+
+* assent verdict → `grantPermission(a, c)`
+* dissent (or withdrawal, see Q-5) → `denyPermission(a, c)` — which fails
+  the purchase with full refunds, exactly the legacy `negativo` branch.
+
+Both verdicts must be observable: reactivegas L2 gives dissent real economic
+content (refund everything), so a rejected proposal may not simply vanish.
+
+### The per-pledge Permesso — direct referente app event (not a proposal)
+
+In legacy code a pledge by anyone other than the referente opened a
+`programmazionePermesso` addressed to the referente alone
+(`fallimento (ut /= r)` — anyone else attempting to answer it is rejected,
+`Eventi/Anagrafe.hs`), whose two outcomes are accept (`pending → accepted`)
+or refuse (refund). This is precisely the Lean pair `acceptPledge` /
+`refusePledge` with guard `col.referente == a`.
+
+It maps to a **direct app event signed by the referente**, not to a proposal,
+for three reasons:
+
+1. **It is not a majority question.** The decision right belongs to one
+   designated person, resolved from application state (`Collection.referente`).
+   Routing it through proposal voting would hand the decision to whoever
+   happens to be admin — a semantics change, and the referente may not even
+   be distinguishable from other admins at the base layer.
+2. **The voters would be wrong.** Base/admin proposals are voted by admins at
+   large; the legacy Permesso is answerable *only* by the named interrogato.
+   kelgroups#30 proposals have no per-designee restriction, and adding one
+   would complicate the base machinery for a case the app already expresses
+   perfectly with signer-based authorization inside `appStep`.
+3. **No escrow semantics belong at the base layer.** Accept/refuse move money
+   between a user's conto and a collection's escrow lists. Keeping them as
+   plain app events means the existing Lean proofs
+   (`pledge_escrow_debit`, `conservation_preserved`) apply verbatim.
+
+## AUTH matrix under sovereign members
+
+One row per event of the Lean `Step`. "Signer" is the CESR key submitted
+with the event, resolved to the authoring user `a` (see
+[Identity resolution](#identity-resolution)). Every row's authorization
+condition is exactly the Lean guard; the channel column says how the event
+reaches the fold. The final column is the row's **declaration meaning**
+under the operator principle: *the machine may gate its own acts, but it
+must never lie about physical events that have already happened*.
+**Authorization** declares an act the machine may refuse; **Attestation**
+records a physical event that already happened; **Mixed** does both. The
+classification is part of the interface contract kelgroups #28 freezes
+against, not a gloss.
+
+| Lean event | authorized signer(s) | additional guard | channel | declaration meaning |
+| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
+| `addUser a u` | proposing admin | target not yet a user | base enactment (`IntroduceMember`) via `appOnBase` | **Authorization** |
+| `electResponsabile a u` | proposing admin | target a user, not yet responsabile | base enactment (`ChangeRoles` +admin) via `appOnBase` | **Authorization** |
+| `removeResponsabile a u` | proposing admin | target currently responsabile | base enactment (`ChangeRoles` −admin, or `RemoveMember`) via `appOnBase` | **Authorization** |
+| `openPurchase a c` | responsabile | fresh collection id | direct app event | **Authorization** |
+| `grantPermission a c` | verdict attribution (proposing admin) | collection exists | #30 app-proposal enactment (assent verdict) | **Authorization** |
+| `denyPermission a c` | verdict attribution (proposing admin) | collection exists | #30 app-proposal enactment (dissent/withdrawal verdict) | **Authorization** |
+| `deposit a u v` | responsabile, `a ≠ u` | `v ≥ 0`, target a user | direct app event | **Attestation** |
+| `withdraw a u v` | responsabile, `a ≠ u` | `bal u ≥ v`, target a user | direct app event | **Authorization** |
+| `transferCassa a f v` | responsabile, `a ≠ f` | `f` responsabile, `v > 0` | direct app event | **Attestation** |
+| `pledge a u c v` | see Q-1: responsabile today; epic ruling adds self-service (`a = u`) | `v > 0`, `bal u ≥ v`, no prior pledge by `u` in `c` | direct app event | **Authorization** |
+| `acceptPledge a u c` | referente only (`col.referente = a`) | pending pledge by `u` | direct app event | **Authorization** |
+| `refusePledge a u c` | referente only | pending pledge by `u` | direct app event | **Authorization** |
+| `correctPledge a u c v'` | referente only | accepted pledge by `u`, `v' ≥ 0`, no overdraft | direct app event | **Authorization** |
+| `closePurchase a c` | referente only | permitted, zero pending pledges | direct app event | **Mixed** |
+| `failPurchase a c` | referente only | zero pending pledges | direct app event | **Authorization** |
+
+Notes on specific rows:
+
+* **Deposits and withdrawals stay responsabile-only** (`a ≠ u` mirrors the
+  legacy `fallimento (r == u)` in `Eventi/Accredito.hs`; L5 double entry,
+  `deposit_double_entry` / `withdraw_double_entry`). Their declaration
+  meanings differ: `deposit` attests cash that already entered the acting
+  responsabile's cassa, while `withdraw` is accepted and appended before
+  physical cash payout; its declaration is authorization, not attestation
+  of a prior payout. A rejected `withdraw` (the `bal u ≥ v` guard fails)
+  means no cash may be handed over and nothing is appended.
+* **Transfers are attestations.** `transferCassa` is receiver-signed: the
+  receiver signs testimony for cash they received after the physical
+  cassa-to-cassa handover. Lean fixes the direction — `transferCassa a f v`
+  moves `v` out of `f`'s cassa into author `a`'s (`Step.lean`), so the
+  author is the receiver and the event records a transfer that already
+  happened rather than licensing a future one.
+* **Closure is mixed.** `closePurchase` discharges the escrow and attests
+  that the supplier was paid by the referente — one declaration doing both
+  jobs: it ends the collection, releasing the escrow the pledges built up,
+  and records the physical settlement. The machine side is the cassa sign
+  convention above: closing debits the collected total from the
+  referente's cassa
+  (`bump s.casse col.referente (-(sumPledges col.accepted))`), which is
+  exactly why a negative cassa is the ordinary operating state of an
+  active referente, not an error and not a debt to chase.
+* **Cassa sign convention.** `casse u` records what cassiere `u` owes the
+  group: Positive means they physically hold group money, and Negative means
+  the group owes them. The machine corroborates the convention: `deposit`
+  raises the receiving cassiere's cassa (`bump s.casse a v`), `withdraw`
+  lowers the acting cassiere's (`bump s.casse a (-v)`), and `closePurchase`
+  lowers the referente's by the collected total
+  (`bump s.casse col.referente (-(sumPledges col.accepted))`) — so a Negative
+  cassa is a normal operating state for an active referente, not an error
+  and not a debt to chase.
+* **On-behalf declarations** (responsabile authors for a keyless or present
+  user) are legal wherever the matrix says "responsabile": the signer context
+  supplies the author, the event names the target. This preserves the legacy
+  model where users do not sign (`Types.lean`: "users do not sign,
+  responsabili declare on their behalf").
+* **Self-service pledges** are demanded by the sovereign-members ruling but
+  conflict with today's Lean AUTH guard, which requires a responsabile author
+  on *every* successful step (`authorizedStep`, `step_authorized`). Per the
+  ticket constraints this is recorded as **Q-1**, not silently resolved.
+* **Referente-only rows** authorize inside `appStep` by comparing the
+  resolved signer against `col.referente` — application state, not a base
+  role. A responsabile who is not the referente fails these rows, matching
+  the legacy `solo <referente> può chiudere` checks in
+  `Eventi/Impegno.hs`.
+
+## The AppLogic interface contract
+
+This section states what reactivegas needs from kelgroups#28, aligned with
+the sketch in that ticket:
+
+```haskell
+data GroupEvent e = Base BaseEvent | App e
+data GroupState s = GroupState { members, pendingProposals, appState :: s }
+
+data AppLogic e s = AppLogic
+    { appInitial :: s
+    , appStep   :: GroupView -> Signer -> s -> e -> Either AppError s
+    , appOnBase :: GroupView -> BaseOutcome -> s -> s
+    }
+```
+
+For reactivegas:
+
+* `e` = `Reactivegas.Event` (all 15 events, interface events included);
+* `s` = `Reactivegas.State`;
+* `appInitial` = `State.init founderId`;
+* `appStep` **is** the Lean `step`, with `none` mapped to `Left`;
+  validation and folding share it, so acceptance-time rejection and replay
+  can never disagree (the #28 invariant).
+
+Requirements reactivegas places on the generalized API:
+
+1. **Distinct event/state types.** Today's `AppFold a = a -> a -> a`
+   (`KelGroups.Fold`) conflates event and fold result; reactivegas needs
+   `GroupEvent e` / `GroupState s` with independent parameters, as sketched
+   above. The current `foldGroup` also ignores the signer when folding app
+   events; `appStep` must receive it.
+2. **Signer + role context.** `Signer` is the submitting member's CESR key;
+   `GroupView` must expose, read-only: the member map keyed by CESR key, each
+   member's roles (so the app can evaluate `isResponsabile` = holds an admin
+   role), and enough information to resolve identities (see below).
+3. **Rejection before append.** `validateEvent` runs `appStep` and refuses
+   the event on `Left`; a refused event never enters the KEL. This is what
+   makes domain-invalid events (an overdraft, a duplicate pledge, a
+   non-referente closure) impossible *in the record*, not merely corrected
+   after the fact — the improvement over the legacy `fallimento` logging
+   described in [Solvency](#solvency-insolvency-is-banned).
+4. **Base→app observation with provenance.** `appOnBase` delivers each
+   enacted base transition in KEL order, exactly once, total (the base never
+   rolls back). `BaseOutcome` must carry the *proposer* of the enacted
+   proposal (`PendingProposal.proposer` already stores it), because the Lean
+   interface events name an author `a`.
+5. **Verdict delivery (#30).** An enacted app-scoped proposal injects its
+   agreed payload as an ordinary app event at the enactment point; a
+   rejected/expired/withdrawn app-scoped proposal injects the denial payload.
+   Both carry KEL provenance so replay reproduces identical app state —
+   reactivegas dissent moves money (`denyPermission` refunds), so an
+   unobservable dissent would corrupt the books.
+
+### Interface events, one-to-one
+
+Every Lean event the substrate produces (rather than a member directly
+signing) with its delivery mechanism:
+
+| interface event | produced by | delivered through |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| `addUser a u` | `IntroduceMember` enactment | `appOnBase` (`BaseOutcome`: member introduced) |
+| `electResponsabile a u` | `ChangeRoles` adding an admin role, enactment | `appOnBase` (`BaseOutcome`: roles changed) |
+| `removeResponsabile a u` | `ChangeRoles` removing the admin role, or `RemoveMember`, enactment | `appOnBase` (`BaseOutcome`: roles changed / member removed) |
+| `grantPermission a c` | assent verdict on the purchase-approval question | #30 verdict injection (`App` event) |
+| `denyPermission a c` | dissent, expiry or withdrawal verdict on the same question | #30 verdict injection (`App` event) |
+
+All ten remaining events arrive as directly signed `App` events (see the
+AUTH matrix). Inside `appOnBase`, the app applies the corresponding Lean
+transition — sharing `step`'s code path, since the provenance guarantees
+(base validated the proposal; the target held the role) satisfy the guards,
+and replay determinism demands the same arithmetic as acceptance.
+
+### Identity resolution
+
+Lean identifiers are `UserId := Nat`; kelgroups identities are CESR keys.
+The contract needs one explicit binding rule, frozen in #28:
+
+* the app maintains a registry `CESR key ↦ UserId`, appended when
+  `addUser` enacts (see Q-4 for how the number is chosen). For a keyless
+  utente the registered key is the handle carried by their
+  `IntroduceMember` proposal — a key they do not hold, so no signature
+  ever names it; the entry serves the base membership record and
+  rendering, never the `Signer` direction (see
+  [Role mapping](#role-mapping));
+* `appStep` resolves the `Signer` key through this registry before applying
+  the guard; an unregistered signer is `Left` (equivalent to the base's own
+  `NotAMember`);
+* `GroupView` supplies the lookup direction `UserId ↦ key` for rendering.
+
+## Solvency: insolvency is banned
+
+The 2026-08-25 amendment bans insolvency outright. In the Lean machine this
+is theorem-grade: solvency guards reject every debit that would overdraw an
+account (withdrawal beyond balance, pledge beyond balance, correction beyond
+the corrected balance) and require positive pledge amounts, so refunds can
+never push anyone under either; `reach_solvent` and `not_insolvent_of_reach`
+prove insolvency unreachable from boot ([State machine](state-machine.md),
+L7).
+
+In the kelgroups flow the guard sits in **`appStep`, executed by
+`validateEvent` at submission time**: a refused debit returns `Left` and is
+never appended to the KEL. There is no "recorded with warning" middle ground
+— that middle ground is exactly what the legacy reactors had
+(`accredita` returned the negative resulting balance and merely logged
+"il credito non copre l'operazione", `Eventi/Accredito.hs` and
+`Eventi/Impegno.hs`).
+
+Client experience of a refused overdraft:
+
+1. the client (wasm core mirroring `step`) pre-checks locally and typically
+   refuses at the UI before anything is sent;
+2. if submitted regardless, the server runs `appStep`, gets `Left`, rejects
+   synchronously with an `AppError` naming the violated guard (e.g.
+   insufficient balance for the pledge);
+3. nothing is appended: no event, no state change, no log entry in the KEL.
+   The proposer sees the refusal immediately and may resubmit a smaller
+   amount; no other member observes anything.
+
+Because rejection happens before append, the KEL contains only transitions
+that were valid when accepted, and replay re-runs the same `appStep` on the
+same prefixes — acceptance and replay cannot disagree (#28 property).
+
+## Vote-machine composition
+
+`grantPermission` and `denyPermission` are valid only when **provably
+vote-derived**. Responsabile authorship alone does not grant or deny
+permission. The **composition theorem** states the cross-machine requirement.
+The economic machine consumes `grantPermission` and `denyPermission` **only
+when the vote machine emitted them**. The composed model has **no direct
+unilateral-responsabile route** to either transition.
+
+The kelgroups#30 vote-result interface exposes the enacted verdict's
+**identity**, the **question** it answers, and its KEL **provenance**. It
+serves **two consumers**: purchase approval and voted comune backdonation.
+
+Status: **enforced: PROVED-IN-MODEL**.
+This status covers the two Lean models only: the paolino/kelgroups
+implementation must mirror the verdict interface and composition theorem;
+the proof is not yet end-to-end.
+
+Reactivegas#54 owns the independent vote machine in `lean/KelGroups/`, the
+composition theorem, and the shared two-consumer verdict interface. It is a
+sub-issue of #43 in the "Reactivegas on kelgroups" milestone and changes the
+model in this repository, not paolino/kelgroups.
+
+The layering is deliberately one-way: `lean/KelGroups/` **imports nothing
+from** `Reactivegas`. `lean/Reactivegas/` is the application and **may import
+KelGroups**. There is **no separate bridge module**: composition lives in
+Reactivegas, where both models are visible.
+
+**Structural composition is evaluated first.** The permission events carry
+the enacted verdict.
+
+## Out of scope, inherited constraints
+
+* Legacy modules with no Lean counterpart — `Eventi/Voci.hs`,
+  `Eventi/Ordine.hs` (order lines attached to purchases),
+  `Eventi/Sincronizzatore.hs`, `Eventi/Logger.hs` — are outside this
+  mapping; they were not extracted in #45 and are not requirements on
+  kelgroups.
+* Multi-gruppo is multiple server instances for the MVP (epic ruling); no
+  cross-group protocol is designed here.
+* Shared infrastructure (default devshell, CI workflows, flake pins) is
+  operator-owned; this document imposes nothing on it.
+
+## Open questions
+
+Each item below needs an operator ruling; none is resolved by editing the
+Lean machine in this ticket.
+
+### Q-1 — Self-service pledges vs the Lean AUTH guard
+
+The sovereign-members ruling makes member-authored pledges ("self-service")
+legal, but `step_authorized` proves every successful step is
+responsabile-authored, and `pledge`'s six-way guard opens with
+`isResponsabile s a` (`Step.lean`, `pledge_guard_inv`). Honouring the ruling
+therefore requires a semantics change, which this ticket may not make.
+**Recommendation:** a minimal follow-up amendment to the Lean spec allowing
+`isResponsabile s a || a == u` for `pledge` only — the solvency, uniqueness
+and conservation proofs survive unchanged because the monetary part of the
+guard does not mention `a` — landed *before* the Haskell core (child 3)
+freezes its implementation. The alternative reading (introduce every member
+as an admin, making everyone a responsabile) is rejected: it collapses the
+role distinction the epic explicitly keeps and changes the voting base of
+every future proposal.
+
+### Q-2 — Expelling a member who owes or is owed money
+
+Legacy never removed ordinary users (`EsternoAnagrafico` has no such event;
+only responsabile revocation exists, and L4 handles its economics).
+kelgroups offers `RemoveMember` outright. Removing a member holding a
+nonzero conto, or with live pledges inside others' collections, has no
+legacy-defined meaning. **Recommendation:** restrict expulsion to
+economically settled members (zero conto, no live pledges), enforced as a
+reactivegas requirement on the operator runbook plus an explicit
+`appOnBase` policy that treats a violating removal as a bug (loud failure),
+until an operator rules otherwise.
+
+### Q-3 — Pending questions opened by a deposed admin
+
+Legacy cancelled the eliminated responsabile's open questions at revocation
+(`reattoreAssenso` on `EventoEliminazioneResponsabile`,
+`Eventi/Anagrafe.hs`). kelgroups' current fold leaves a removed member's
+pending proposals in place (approvals accumulate; the majority threshold is
+recomputed from current admins). Reactivegas needs deposed-proposer
+questions resolved, not orphaned. **Recommendation:** kelgroups#28/#30
+specify cancellation-at-enactment: when a proposal's proposer loses admin
+(or membership), the proposal is removed and — if it was an app-scoped
+purchase-approval question — delivered to the app as a denial verdict, so
+the L2 refund path triggers exactly as in legacy.
+
+### Q-4 — Key→UserId assignment
+
+The identity registry of [Identity resolution](#identity-resolution) needs a
+deterministic rule for choosing the `Nat` given a CESR key. **Recommendation:**
+a monotonic counter in app state, advanced by `addUser`; replay reproduces
+it trivially, and the numbers never appear in the base layer. Freezing this
+in #28 avoids ad-hoc digests leaking into the economic state.
+
+### Q-5 — Withdrawal of a purchase-approval proposal
+
+Legacy let the promoter close their own assenso question early
+(`EventoFallimentoAssenso`, "rinuncia", restricted to the promoter by
+`fallimento (ur /= r)`), and the closing ran the *negative* continuation —
+for a purchase, full refunds. For #30 parity, a withdrawn app-scoped
+proposal must be observable as a denial verdict, not silently deleted.
+**Recommendation:** support proposer-withdrawal in #30 delivering the same
+denial payload as dissent; until then, clients express withdrawal as a
+campaign for dissent, which is semantically equivalent but slower.
+
